Recommended shape: v2 is written to `C:/nscrev/reports/repo-durability-and-reset-design-v2-20260918.md`. It keeps one reset repo (the game repo, with the tools moved in) plus four private archive repos that never take part in a reset: `nsc-ops`, `nsc-control` for `.assistant-control` (whitelist only), `nsc-reports` and `nsc-home`. Every archive repo's git dir sits outside its tree, and one 15-minute backup job verifies each push. Live data shows the pipeline itself writes full `.sync-*` Unity clones inside `.assistant-control`, so the whitelist is required, not just prevention.
Worst-case loss window: 15 minutes for anything committed or written to a record or log, candidate commits included (was 24 hours). It counts only while the backup-age item in the viewer header is green, and the run in flight (up to 60 minutes of provider spend) stays uncovered.
Cheapest first step: `git bundle create <OneDrive>\nsc-<date>.bundle --all` on the game repo, then confirm OneDrive shows it synced. It takes minutes, covers all 133 local-only branches and 117 non-branch refs, and is not done yet — no nsc-named bundle is in the top level of OneDrive today.
v1 blocking findings closed: 9 of 9. B1–B8 are redesigned and B9 is moot under D1; W1, W2, W3, W5 and W6 are also answered. The undo work (migration steps 8–9) is re-estimated from two fix branches to six.
